        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You came into hospital in labour at 39 weeks. Your baby's heart rate monitor (CTG) showed signs of distress. Because of this, you needed an emergency operation to deliver your baby (emergency lower segment caesarean section).\n\nAfter the birth, you had heavy bleeding (primary postpartum haemorrhage) of around 1200 ml. This was caused by the womb not contracting as it should (uterine atony)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "To treat the heavy bleeding, the team used:\n- a medicine to help the womb contract (oxytocin)\n- another medicine to help the womb contract (ergometrine)\n- bimanual compression\n\nYou did not need to go back to theatre. The bleeding was controlled.\n\nA blood test on day 1 after the operation showed your haemoglobin had fallen. Because of this, an iron tablet was started on day 1 after the operation.\n\nYou were also started on a daily injection to reduce the risk of blood clots (enoxaparin)."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "- A community midwife handover has been arranged.\n- Your GP and health visitor have been informed.\n- You have a postnatal check with your GP at 6 weeks."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
